<article class="env-lemma" id="0G7P">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0G7P">Lemma <span data-tag="0G7P">36.6.9</span>.</a> Let $X$ be a scheme. Let $Z \subset X$ be a closed subset which can locally be cut out by a Koszul regular sequence having $c$ elements. Then $\mathcal{H}^ i_ Z(\mathcal{F}) = 0$ for $i \not= c$ for every flat, quasi-coherent $\mathcal{O}_ X$-module $\mathcal{F}$. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      By the description of $R\mathcal{H}_ Z(\mathcal{F})$ given in Lemma <a href="/tag/0G7M" data-tag="0G7M">36.6.7</a> this boils down to the following algebra statement: given a ring $R$, a Koszul regular sequence $f_1, \ldots , f_ c \in R$, and a flat $R$-module $M$, the extended alternating Čech complex $M \to \bigoplus \nolimits _{i_0} M_{f_{i_0}} \to \bigoplus \nolimits _{i_0 < i_1} M_{f_{i_0}f_{i_1}} \to \ldots \to M_{f_1 \ldots f_ c}$ from More on Algebra, Section <a href="/tag/0G6F" data-tag="0G6F">15.30</a> only has cohomology in degree $c$. By More on Algebra, Lemma <a href="/tag/0G6L" data-tag="0G6L">15.32.1</a> we obtain the desired vanishing for the extended alternating Čech complex of $R$. Since the complex for $M$ is obtained by tensoring this with the flat $R$-module $M$ (More on Algebra, Lemma <a href="/tag/0G6H" data-tag="0G6H">15.30.2</a>) we conclude. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>